Lichen Sclerosus (Mild Extragenital)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Asymptomatic, atrophic, porcelain-white plaques on the trunk or shoulders

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-024
Reason for Visit: Acute panic and severe psychological distress following the discovery of an unusual skin lesion, requiring exclusion of rapidly spreading soft tissue infection or acute vasculitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate a new, unusual cutaneous plaque discovered by the patient that caused extreme distress. Immediate clinical examination was required to rule out acute localized scleroderma (morphea) with impending joint restriction, severe fungal infection, or a necrotic vasculitic lesion prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I was changing my shirt and noticed this dead, white, shiny skin on my shoulder. It looks completely abnormal, like my flesh is dying, and I am terrified it's spreading.
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with isolated pale plaques on non-mucosal skin. The patient discovered the lesion accidentally tonight and immediately developed severe anxiety regarding skin necrosis or a severe tropical infection. The lesion itself is completely asymptomatic—no pain, itch, or burning. The patient has been rubbing the area vigorously, causing surrounding reactive erythema, which escalated the fear of rapid spread.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: High health anxiety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 66 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, but overtly anxious, fixated on the shoulder.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam reveals asymptomatic, atrophic, porcelain-white plaques on the trunk or shoulders (specifically, the left posterior shoulder). The skin feels slightly thinned ("cigarette paper" texture) but is not deeply indurated or bound down to underlying fascia. Surrounding skin shows mild, blanching erythema consistent with mechanical rubbing by the patient. No warmth, no purulence, no regional lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Normal residential environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current topical or oral medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Extragenital Lichen Sclerosus (Mild) with secondary health anxiety - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Morphea (Localized Scleroderma) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The plaque is superficially atrophic and wrinkled rather than deeply thickened, hard, and firmly bound to underlying tissue.

Tinea Corporis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete lack of an active, scaling, erythematous border and absence of pruritus.

Cutaneous Necrosis/Vasculitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal capillary refill in surrounding tissue, lack of pain, and absence of dark eschar or ulceration.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe psychological distress secondary to discovering a porcelain-white cutaneous plaque. Triage appropriately escalated the call to definitively rule out a necrotic or rapidly spreading infectious/vasculitic process that the patient feared. Bedside examination confirmed the lesion was non-infectious, non-necrotic, and superficially atrophic, identifying it as extragenital Lichen Sclerosus. Immediate clinical reassurance was absolutely necessary to stop the patient from continuing to mechanically damage the skin and to de-escalate severe panic, averting an inappropriate late-night emergency department presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the benign, non-infectious nature of the plaque. Advised to cease rubbing the area.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a plain, unscented moisturizer over the area once daily and completely refrain from touching or inspecting it otherwise. By stopping the mechanical rubbing, the red, irritated ring will vanish by morning, proving to you that the lesion is not aggressively spreading.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment in 2-3 weeks for formal dermatological diagnosis and potential topical steroid prescription.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to a GP clinic or Emergency Department if the area breaks open into a bleeding ulcer, if it becomes extremely hot and painful to the touch, or if you develop a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal biopsy if required; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: This is a slow-moving, chronic autoimmune skin condition where the skin thins out and loses color. It is not life-threatening and does not indicate skin death.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical review if you notice new, similar white patches developing rapidly in other areas, particularly in the genital region.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The white patch is just thinned skin caused by a localized immune reaction. The redness around it is entirely from you scratching and rubbing it tonight in a panic.
Actionable Steps: Stop rubbing the shoulder immediately. Use a gentle moisturizer. Your GP can prescribe a special cream to stop it from slowly growing over the coming months.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Lichen Sclerosus (benign thinned skin plaque). No signs of infection, necrosis, or spread. Stop rubbing the area. Safe to rest. See GP in 2 weeks.

SMS EDUCATION

Stop rubbing the white patch to let the redness settle. Moisturize gently. Go to ED only if the skin breaks into a bleeding ulcer or becomes extremely painful.